1
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:08,833
Why bother standardizing the inputs?

2
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:14,516
The first reason is scale, because a feature in the thousands drowns out one that's near one.

3
00:00:14,516 --> 00:00:21,166
The second is numerics, because mixing huge and tiny numbers throws away floating-point precision.

4
00:00:21,166 --> 00:00:22,711
Here's how bad it can get.

5
00:00:22,711 --> 00:00:27,763
Take one feature that runs from one hundred to two hundred, plus an intercept column,

6
00:00:27,763 --> 00:00:30,616
and those two columns point almost the same way.

7
00:00:30,616 --> 00:00:36,083
Kappa comes out around six hundred thousand, which means more than a million gradient steps.

8
00:00:36,083 --> 00:00:41,866
Subtract the mean and divide by the spread, and kappa is exactly one.

9
00:00:41,866 --> 00:00:43,397
Now for a subtler reason.

10
00:00:43,397 --> 00:00:48,418
A ReLU unit bends where its input crosses zero, at minus the bias over the weight.

11
00:00:48,418 --> 00:00:53,316
When both of those are standard normal, that kink follows a Cauchy distribution.

12
00:00:53,316 --> 00:00:58,337
It has infinite variance, and yet half the time the kink lands within one of zero,

13
00:00:58,337 --> 00:01:02,933
and ninety-one percent of the time within seven.

14
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:06,002
Now put the data between one hundred and two hundred.

15
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:10,461
A kink lands in that range with a chance of about zero point one six percent.

16
00:01:10,461 --> 00:01:18,466
So out of a thousand hidden units, only one or two bend inside the data, and the rest are just flat or straight there.

17
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:20,095
And that's the real point.

18
00:01:20,095 --> 00:01:28,400
Unstandardized data wastes the whole nonlinearity, and the same thing happens with tanh and sigmoid units.

19
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:33,484
With no kink inside the data, each unit is either zero or a straight line there.

20
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:37,233
So all thousand units add up to just a few rank-one pieces.

21
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:42,635
Take fifty data points, and the raw features have only three nonzero singular values,

22
00:01:42,635 --> 00:01:45,733
while the standardized ones have all fifty.

23
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:47,995
That gives us a cheap debugging trick.

24
00:01:47,995 --> 00:01:53,600
If a model seems oddly weak, check the rank of its feature matrix.

25
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:55,949
So how should we initialize the weights?

26
00:01:55,949 --> 00:02:00,824
All zeros fails, because everything gets multiplied by zero and nothing ever moves.

27
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:05,287
The key observation is that one layer's outputs are the next layer's inputs.

28
00:02:05,287 --> 00:02:09,833
So we want every layer's inputs to start out looking standardized.

29
00:02:09,833 --> 00:02:16,930
A unit adds up its inputs, each with variance one, so the output variance is the fan-in times the weight variance.

30
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:23,653
That tells us to give the weights a variance of one over the fan-in, and then the output keeps variance one.

31
00:02:23,653 --> 00:02:27,100
This is Xavier initialization.

32
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:29,559
Does that actually keep the signal alive?

33
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:36,577
Let's push standardized inputs through ten ReLU layers of width two hundred fifty-six, with three choices of weights.

34
00:02:36,577 --> 00:02:42,833
Standard normal weights blow the signal up by about two orders of magnitude at every single layer.

35
00:02:42,833 --> 00:02:50,451
Xavier suits a linear layer, but a ReLU zeroes half of its outputs, so the signal halves at each layer and fades away.

36
00:02:50,451 --> 00:02:52,324
He initialization fixes that.

37
00:02:52,324 --> 00:02:58,100
Since half the inputs are dead, it doubles the variance, and the signal stays level.

38
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:04,672
Glorot initialization averages the fan-in and the fan-out, to balance the forward and backward passes,

39
00:03:04,672 --> 00:03:07,185
and with equal widths it's just Xavier.

40
00:03:07,185 --> 00:03:09,118
Biases are easy by comparison.

41
00:03:09,118 --> 00:03:15,533
You start them at zero, or at a small constant like zero point zero one.

